menders and Search Tools\nL135: \nL136: [Input] Link to Influence Flower\nL137: \nL138: Influence Flower (cite39†What are Influence Flowers?†influencemap.cmlab.dev )\nL139: \nL140: [Input] Core recommender toggle\nL141: \nL142: CORE Recommender (cite40†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\nL143: \nL144:   * Author\nL145:   * Venue\nL146:   * Institution\nL147:   * Topic\nL148: \nL149: [Input] About arXivLabs\nL150: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL151: \nL152: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL153: \nL154: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL155: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite41†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL156: \nL157: cite42†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite43†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL158: \nL159: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite44†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL160: cite45†About†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L161: \nL162: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n07_Pallo.dvi (https://acta.bibl.u-szeged.hu/12796/1/Pallo_2006_ActaCybernetica.pdf)\nciteturn9505view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://acta.bibl.u-szeged.hu/12796/1/Pallo_2006_ActaCybernetica.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 426\nL0@P0: Acta Cybernetica 17 (2006) 799–810.\nL1@P0: Rotational tree structures on binary trees and\nL2@P0: triangulations∗\nL3@P0: Jean Marcel Pallo†\nL4@P0: Abstract\nL5@P0: A rotation in a binary tree is a simple and local restructuring technique\nL6@P0: commonly used in computer science. We propose in this paper three restric\u0002tions on the general rotation operation. We study the case when only leftmost\nL7@P0: rotations are permitted, which corresponds to a natural flipping on polygon\nL8@P0: triangulations. The resulting combinatorial structure is a tree structure with\nL9@P0: the root as the greatest element. We exhibit an efficient algorithm for com\u0002puting the join of two trees and the minimum number of leftmost rotations\nL10@P0: necessary to transform one tree into the other.\nL11@P0: Keywords: Binary trees; Rotation; Distance; Lattice; Algorithms\nL12@P0: 1 Introduction\nL13@P0: Rotation is one of the most common operations for restructuring binary trees. It\nL14@P0: has the advantage of altering the depths of some of the nodes in the tree while\nL15@P0: preserving the symmetric order of all the nodes. Thus rotation is commonly used\nL16@P0: in a variety of algorithms for maintaining binary search trees with a good amortized\nL17@P0: behavior [10, 24, 28].\nL18@P0: The combinatorial properties of binary trees under the rotation operation have\nL19@P0: been studied for thirty years [27]. In [17] we have shown that a directed version\nL20@P0: of the rotation graph of binary trees with n nodes is a lattice, known as the nth\nL21@P0: Tamari lattice. This corresponds to the case when only left rotations are permitted\nL22@P0: in the binary tree transformation. Over the last ten years, Tamari lattices have\nL23@P0: often been used as examples to illustrate algebraic theories [1, 3, 16, 25].\nL24@P0: Initially, Tamari lattices were orderings of parenthesizations of words. But\nL25@P0: nowadays they can be described in other ways via the well-known bijections between\nL26@P0: families of Catalan combinatorial objects. A system that is isomorphic to Tamari\nL27@P0: lattices is that of triangulations of a convex polygon related by the diagonal flip\nL28@P0: operation. This is the transformation that converts one triangulation into another\nL29@P0: ∗The results reported in this paper were presented at the 11th International Conference AFL\nL30@P0: 2005 (Automata and Formal Languages) held at Dobog´ok˝o, Hungary, May 17–20.\nL31@P0: †LE2I, UMR 5158, Universit´e de Bourgogne, BP 47870, F21078 DIJON-Cedex, France, E-mail:\nL32@P0: pallo@u-bourgogne.fr\nL33@P0-1: 799800 Jean Marcel Pallo\nL34@P1: 1 4 4\nL35@P1: 2 35\nL36@P1: 6 7\nL37@P1: 1\nL38@P1: 2 3\nL39@P1: 5\nL40@P1: 6 7\nL41@P1: 5\nL42@P1: 6\nL43@P1: 7\nL44@P1: 4\nL45@P1: 3\nL46@P1: 2\nL47@P1: 11\nL48@P1: 2\nL49@P1: 3\nL50@P1: 4\nL51@P1: 5\nL52@P1: 6\nL53@P1: 7\nL54@P1: Figure 1: A triangulation diagonal flip and its corresponding binary tree rotation\nL55@P1: by removing a diagonal in the triangulation and adding the diagonal that subdivides\nL56@P1: the resulting quadrilateral on the opposite way [8, 9, 26] (see Fig. 1).\nL57@P1: In 1982, Culik and Wood defined the rotation distance between two binary trees\nL58@P1: with the same number of leaves as the minimum number of rotations necessary to\nL59@P1: transform one tree into the other [4]. Using the classical bijection between binary\nL60@P1: trees with n internal nodes and triangulations of (n+2)- gons, the previous distance\nL61@P1: is equivalent to the minimum number of diagonal-flip transformations needed to\nL62@P1: convert one triangulation of a polygon into another. There remains today an open\nL63@P1: problem whether the rotation distance can be computed in polynomial time.\nL64@P1: Therefore it seems natural to consider special instances of rotation transforma\u0002tions in order to obtain simpler operations [12, 24]. In [2] the rotation operation is\nL65@P1: limited to the case where the leftmost subtree is constrained to be a leaf. In [5, 6,\nL66@P1: 11, 22] the authors only allow rotations at nodes along the right arm of a tree.\nL67@P1: The current paper belongs to this appoach. We consider the problem by limiting\nL68@P1: the general rotation operation to the restricted version where only leftmost rotations\nL69@P1: on trees are allowed. We obtain a tree structure which is a join-semilattice with the\nL70@P1: root as the greatest element. An efficient algorithm computes the corresponding\nL71@P1: restricted rotation distance. This algorithm is constructive: it builds a sequence of\nL72@P1: leftmost rotations transforming one tree into the other.\nL73@P1: Clearly, the restricted rotation distance defined above is bounded below by the\nL74@P1-2: usual rotation distance for which no efficient algorithm is known to compute itRotational tree structures on binary trees and triangulations 801\nL75@P2: exactly. However, this restricted rotation distance is a weak approximation of the\nL76@P2: usual rotation distance. A better approximation can be found in [21, 23]. This new\nL77@P2: metric can be considered as a way of measuring the difference in shape between\nL78@P2: two binary trees.\nL79@P2: 2 Definitions and terminology\nL80@P2: Let us denote by \u0003 (respectively ✷) internal nodes (respectively leaves) of a binary\nL81@P2: tree. Let TL (respectively TR) denote the left (respectively right) subtree of a binary\nL82@P2: tree T (the order is significant). Thus we can write T = \u0003TLTR in Polish notation,\nL83@P2: i.e. by traversing T in preorder (visit the root and then the left and right subtrees\nL84@P2: recursively). The weight |T | of a binary tree T is the number of leaves of T . Let Bn\nL85@P2: denote the set of binary trees with n internal nodes (and thus with n + 1 leaves).\nL86@P2: The leaves of T ∈ Bn are numbered from 1 to n + 1 by a preorder traversal of T\nL87@P2: (i.e. from left to right). The left (respectively right) arm of T ∈ Bn is the path\nL88@P2: from the root of T to its first (respectively (n + 1)th) leaf. The mirror image T\u0002 of\nL89@P2: T is recursively defined by T\u0002 = \u0003T\nL90@P2: \u0003RT\nL91@P2: \u0003L and ∼\nL92@P2: ✷ = ✷. Let us define 0n =(\u0003✷)n✷\nL93@P2: (respectively 1n = \u0003n✷n+1) the tree of Bn where every internal node has a leaf as\nL94@P2: a left (respectively right) child.\nL95@P2: In this paper we use the representation of binary trees via weight sequences\nL96@P2: introduced in [17]. This coding is defined as follows. Given T ∈ Bn, the weight\nL97@P2: sequence of T is the integer sequence wT =(wT (1),...,wT (n)) where wT (i) is\nL98@P2: the weight of the largest subtree of T whose last leaf is the ith leaf (see Fig. 2).\nL99@P2: The usual left-rotation → on Bn is defined as follows. A tree T ∈ Bn being given,\nL100@P2: it associates a tree T \u0003 obtained by replacing some subtree \u0003T1 \u0003 T2T3 of T by the\nL101@P2: \u0003✷\u0003\u0003✷✷\u0003✷✷ \u0003\u0003✷\u0003✷✷\u0003✷✷ \u0003\u0003\u0003✷✷✷\u0003✷✷ \u0003\u0003\u0003\u0003✷✷✷✷✷\nL102@P2: 1121 1131 1231 1234\nL103@P2: 2\nL104@P2: 1\nL105@P2: 3\nL106@P2: 5\nL107@P2: 4\nL108@P2: root\nL109@P2: 2\nL110@P2: 3\nL111@P2: 5\nL112@P2: 4\nL113@P2: root\nL114@P2: 1\nL115@P2: 2\nL116@P2: 3\nL117@P2: 5\nL118@P2: 4\nL119@P2: root\nL120@P2: 1\nL121@P2: 2\nL122@P2: 3\nL123@P2: 5\nL124@P2: 4\nL125@P2: root\nL126@P2: 1\nL127@P2: ❀❀ ❀\nL128@P2-3: Figure 2: Three lefmost left-rotations in B4 and the corresponding flips in T6802 Jean Marcel Pallo\nL129@P3: subtree \u0003\u0003T1T2T3. Let −1 → denote the right-rotation and let ∗\nL130@P3: → denote the reflexive\nL131@P3: transitive closure of →. The usual rotation distance between T and T \u0003 ∈ Bn is the\nL132@P3: fewest number of left- and right-rotations required to convert T into T \u0003\nL133@P3: . We have\nL134@P3: proved in [17] the following characterization: given T,T \u0003 ∈ Bn, we have T ∗\nL135@P3: → T \u0003 iff\nL136@P3: for all i ∈ [1,n]: wT (i) ≤ wT\u0002 (i).\nL137@P3: Let us consider (n + 2)-gons, i.e. convex polygons with n + 2 sides and with a\nL138@P3: distinguished side as the top. We label the other sides from 1 to n+ 1 counterclock\u0002wise. Any triangulation of the (n + 2)-gon has n triangles and n − 1 non-crossing\nL139@P3: diagonals. Let Tn+2 denote the set of triangulations of the (n+ 2)-gon. There is an\nL140@P3: explicit bijection τ between Bn and Tn+2 [23,26]. The top of the (n + 2)-gon τ(T )\nL141@P3: corresponds to the root of the tree T . The ith side of τ(T ) corresponds to the ith\nL142@P3: leaf of T . Diagonals corresponds to internal nodes recursively as follows. If j is the\nL143@P3: last leaf of the left subtree TL of T , then TL corresponds to the (j + 1)-gon having\nL144@P3: edge set {1,...,j} and the right subtree TR corresponds to the (n − j + 2)-gon\nL145@P3: having edge set {j +1,...,n +1} (see Fig. 1 and 2).\nL146@P3: Given some T ∈ Bn with T \u0007= 1n, according to the Polish notation of T , consider\nL147@P3: the leftmost ✷ followed by a \u0003 which respectively are the last leaf of a subtree T1\nL148@P3: and the root of a subtree \u0003T2T3. Thus the root of \u0003T1 \u0003 T2T3 is located on the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2501.10311] The Pop-Stack Operator on Ornamentation Lattices (https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.10311)\nciteturn9505view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.10311\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\n"

